# CSS Display Properties

The `display` property controls how an element participates in layout.

## 1. `display: block`

- Starts on a new line by default.
- Usually takes up the available width of its parent.
- `width` and `height` can be set.

Examples: `<div>`, `<p>`, `<h1>` (by default).

```css
.block {
  display: block;
  width: 200px;
  height: 100px;
}
```

## 2. `display: inline`

- Flows alongside other inline content on the same line when space allows.
- `width` and `height` generally do not control its size.
- Horizontal padding and margins work; vertical padding and margins do not affect surrounding line layout in the same way as block elements.

Example: `<span>` (by default).

```css
.inline {
  display: inline;
}
```

## 3. `display: inline-block`

- Sits alongside other inline content.
- Allows `width` and `height` to be set.
- Useful for elements such as buttons or badges that need specific dimensions while remaining in a row.

```css
.inline-block {
  display: inline-block;
  width: 120px;
  height: 40px;
}
```

## Key takeaway

- `block`: starts on a new line; dimensions can be set.
- `inline`: flows within a line; width and height generally don't control its size.
- `inline-block`: flows within a line and supports explicit width and height.

These are default behaviors, and CSS can change an element's display type.



# CSS Display Property

The CSS `display` property controls how an element participates in page layout.

## 1. Block

- Starts on a new line by default.
- Usually takes up the available width of its parent.
- `width` and `height` can be set.

Examples: `<div>`, `<p>`, `<h1>` by default.

```css
.element {
  display: block;
}
```

## 2. Inline

- Flows alongside other inline content when space allows.
- `width` and `height` generally do not control its size.
- Horizontal padding and margins work, but vertical padding and margins do not affect surrounding line layout in the same way as block elements.

Examples: `<span>` and `<a>` by default.

```css
.element {
  display: inline;
}
```

## 3. Inline-block

- Flows alongside other inline content.
- Allows explicit `width` and `height`.
- Useful for items such as buttons or badges that need specific dimensions while remaining in a row.

```css
.element {
  display: inline-block;
  width: 150px;
  height: 50px;
}
```

## Quick comparison

| Property | New line by default? | Set width and height? |
|---|---|---|
| `block` | Yes | Yes |
| `inline` | No | Generally no |
| `inline-block` | No | Yes |

**Note:** These are default layout behaviors. CSS can change an element's display type.
